WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.620
Teams are turning to ai chatbots when no one else is around at 2am. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 166. A teenager posted on Reddit this week about opening ChatGPT when their mental health felt overwhelming. ChatGPT is a popular artificial intelligence chatbot, meaning a computer program you talk to in regular words and it talks back. The writer lives on a tight budget with no car and no nearby crisis center they could actually reach.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:51.500
A crisis center is a place built for mental health emergencies but it only helps if you can get there. They had no friends or family they could text at midnight. Therapy was too expensive, had long wait times, or would not take their insurance. Even people with insurance often meet therapists who reject that specific plan, or who are not taking new patients.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:01:11.220
Cheaper sliding-scale prices, meaning rates that drop with your income, still felt impossible on a very small fixed income. They depend on buses and trains and could not spend 30 or $40 on a ride share during a crisis. A ride share is a paid car from an app, and that money simply was not there.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:24.880
Picture real help as a locked door. The keys are money, a working insurance card, a ride, or a person who picks up the phone. Without those keys, the door stays shut, no matter how badly you need it open.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:34.760
Opening the chatbot gave them a place to dump racing thoughts. That is like taking off a backpack that has been crushing your shoulders just so you can see what you were carrying.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:46.440
It also helped turn messy feelings into clearer words, almost like translating a language you only have no. The post pushed back against people who shame anyone for using AI this way.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:57.660
The writer said the real choice for some people is chat GPT or nobody. Many rural towns, meaning places far from big cities, have almost no mental health care at all.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:06.880
The writer knows the chatbot is not a licensed therapist, which means it is not a trained professional allowed to treat you. They also know it can make mistakes.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.060
It still helps them put feelings into words or look at the same problem from another angle.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:19.440
The post asks readers to consider what actual options exist before judging someone for using AI on a hard night.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:26.860
Those options are friends, family, insurance, and transportation, and a lot of teens and students do not have them.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:35.720
These tools are quietly filling gaps that human support systems leave open. You already know a baby version of the wording trick from your own phone.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:46.920
When you text something emotional, the keyboard sometimes suggests the next word that matches the mood. A chatbot stretches that same guess across a whole conversation, not just one sentence.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:03:00.880
Step 1. It reads everything you typed. The frustration, the confusion, the extra random details. Step 2. It guesses what kind of reply would fit next. The way a group chat tries to keep a thread alive.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:10.140
Step 3. It chooses words that match patterns for millions of examples of people talking through hard moments. It does not have feelings of its own.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:20.040
Picture a librarian who has read millions of letters people wrote when they were upset. The librarian is not sad with you. They can still spot a pattern like,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:26.980
this person needs their thoughts reflected back, or this person might want a different camera angle on the same problem.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:33.260
That matching can turn a jumbled paragraph into something clear you can actually sit with and reread.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:42.540
Step 4. The longer you chat, the more context it holds. It is like a partner who remembers the last few pages of a notebook you have been writing in together.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:55.400
None of that is a soul inside the machine. It is extremely good auto-complete, stretched from one word to a whole thread. Once you see pattern matching plus a short memory, the mystery gets a lot smaller.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:05.000
If you have chat GPT on your phone, you can watch that memory work in a low stakes way. Describe a homework assignment in a messy out-of-order paragraph.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:17.180
Then send a second message that only says, use what I already told you and make a simple plan. Notice it pulling details from the first note, like those notebook pages, instead of starting from zero.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:32.020
11 Labs just released music version 2.5, a tool that builds original songs from a text description. You type the vibe you want. A chill, low-fi beat, an upbeat pop track, or a song with specific instruments.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:38.420
Then it makes the actual audio, like describing a song to a band that can play anything you name.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:50.320
The company trained it only on licensed music, meaning they had permission to learn from those songs. Think of cooking only from recipes the chefs actually shared, not from a stolen cookbook.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:05:05.360
That detail matters if you ever want to share a track because it avoids using copyrighted songs without permission. In a blind test with nearly 48,000 comparison pairs, listeners chose the new model over the older one more often.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:15.440
A blind test is like tasting two drinks without knowing which is which, then picking the one you like. Anyone with a phone or laptop can try the free tier right now.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:24.360
Go to the 11 Labs website, make a free account if it asks, and type this prompt. Soft acoustic guitar song about summer evenings.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:35.280
Generate a couple of tracks, change one word in the description, and listen for how the sound shifts. You can use it in a web browser, and programmers can also plug it into other apps.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:39.760
There is a free option and a paid one, so beginners can start without paying.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:50.380
Tiny challenge for later. Make one track in the morning and another at night with the exact same prompt. Here how the system interprets the same idea differently each time.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:06:00.000
California just changed the rules. Governor Newsom signed Adam's Law, which sets rules for chatbot companies so they better protect children who use them.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:07.740
It came after concerns about how these tools interact with younger users, and it aims to keep chatbots from causing harm to kids.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:18.840
Companies must add safety features that respond to signs a child might be in distress. Those features are a bit like a smoke alarm for a conversation, listening for trouble, and then reacting.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:24.940
Parents and educators can now point to this law when they ask companies how their tools keep kids safe.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:33.580
Ethan Mollick suggested a shared bank of historical mysteries and untranslated handwritten records, so AI models can try cracking them.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:41.580
He posted that idea on X, noting people often throw AI at math problems while old puzzles could be just as interesting.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:47.600
Some historians are already testing newer models on ancient documents nobody has read in centuries.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:54.220
Researchers writing under the name Generative History feed old handwritten texts into those systems.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:02.680
The shared bank idea would let anyone experiment, like handing the latest model a pile of unsolved crossword puzzles from hundreds of years ago.

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:11.200
